# THE FUSIONS THAT OWE AN EXACT COMPOSED REMAINDER. One row per composed layer whose demand and
# nameplate eliminations are BOTH answered, so `r_composed = F Phi r_parts - e_n + e_d` is a
# closed expression rather than a warning.
#
# ⭐⭐ IT IS A DIFFERENCE AND ITS LEFT OPERAND IS A FILE, WHICH IS THE POINT. A difference whose
#    left side is spelled inline cannot be counted, so no law on algebra/roster.sqlc can govern
#    it. `composition/fusions.sqlc` is the A, `composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc` is the B,
#    and algebra/owed_remainder.sqlc counts them. See composition/carriable.sqlc, which exists
#    for the same reason at the other difference.
#
# ⭐ IT IS THE REMAINDER'S TWIN OF composition/owed_equality.sqlc, WHICH IS THE DEMAND'S. That
#   one suspends on `coalesce(s.quantity, 'demand') = 'demand'` alone. A remainder needs both
#   arms, so the two populations differ and neither substitutes for the other.
#
# ⛔ IT IS ALSO WHAT MAKES `coalesce(e, 0)` SOUND ONE JOIN LATER. Once the suspended fusions are
#   out, an absent elimination means the composer LOOKED and found no double counting, which is
#   the number zero. Inside the suspended set the same absence means nobody looked, and filling
#   it would put a claim in the checker that the document declined to make.
-- composition/fusions.sqlc less composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc.
SELECT f.filing, f.layer
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc)
) s ON s.composition = f.filing AND s.composed_layer = f.layer
WHERE s.composition IS NULL
